The '''Isthmus War''' was a major theater of the [[Great Adonian War]] fought on the [[Isthmus of Balboa]], in [[Palpoa Province]], and along the [[Sinchew]]–[[Mercian Lurandia]] border from 1935 to 1947. The conflict pitted a [[Volisania|Volisanian]]-led coalition comprising [[Balboa]], [[Mercian Lurandia]] (also known as [[New Hwice]]), the [[United Midlands]], [[Breisland]], and [[Mercia]] against a [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]]-led coalition comprising [[Sinchew]], [[Louyang]], and [[Sanrobonia]]. The Isthmus War was the third major conflict fought over the isthmus, following the [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716) and the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899).

The war was initiated by the Volisanian–Balboan assault on [[Sant Cristòfor]] in 1935, which opened the [[Great Adonian War]] in the Lurandian theater. The initial objective was the reconquest of Sant Cristòfor and the [[Balboa Canal]], which had been under Zong control since the Treaty of Sant Llàtzer (1899). The conflict expanded in 1937 when Mercia concluded an agreement with Mercian Lurandia to transfer Palpoa Province in exchange for military action against Sinchewese forces stationed there, drawing Mercian Lurandia and the United Midlands into the war and triggering the entry of Louyang on the Zong side. The [[Lurandian Theater]], fought in the [[Great Lurandian Range]] between the Sinchew–Louyang and Mercian Lurandia–United Midlands–Mercia forces, is treated as a separate article.

The Isthmus War was characterised by the prolonged siege of Sant Cristòfor, which held out for the entire twelve-year duration of the conflict through Zong naval supply lines; the contested Palpoa campaign, in which the province changed hands multiple times; and the 1942 Zong–Sinchew counter-offensive through Palpoa that captured the Balboan interior, including Sant Bart in 1943. The war ended in 1947 with the collapse of the Volisanian fascist government. Palpoa was returned to Sinchew in the post-war settlement.

The conflict is notable for the degree to which it was fought by former colonial territories on behalf of their former metropoles: Sinchew and Louyang were obligated to support Zong as [[Zong Commonwealth|commonwealth constituents]], while Mercian Lurandia was obligated by Mercia and the United Midlands joined under allied pressure. Sanrobonia, an independent ally of Zong, provided the northern staging ground for the 1942 counter-offensive. The war's civilian toll — particularly the estimated 1.8 million deaths in the Balboan interior from famine, aerial bombing, and forced labour — made it one of the deadliest theaters of the Great Adonian War relative to the area affected.

== Background ==

{{main|Second War of the Isthmus|Zong Balboa}}

The [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899) ended with the [[Treaty of Sant Llàtzer]], which transferred sovereignty over Sant Cristòfor and the Balboa Canal to Zong and ceded a strip of southern Balboan territory — organised as [[Palpoa Province]] — to Sinchew. The loss of Sant Cristòfor, the isthmus's principal commercial centre, and the canal, its most important commercial waterway, reduced Balboa to an impoverished rump state and generated a revanchist current in both Balboan and Volisanian politics.

The [[National Volisan Party]], which seized power in Volisania in 1911, made the recovery of Sant Cristòfor and the canal a central objective of its foreign policy. Balboa, under the authoritarian government of [[Rodrigo Velásquez]] and his successors, aligned with Volisanian fascism and committed to the reconquest of the lost territory. The two decades preceding the war saw increasing military cooperation between Volisania and Balboa, including the re-equipment of the Balboan constabulary with Volisanian-supplied weapons in violation of the Treaty of Sant Llàtzer.

The Zong territory of [[Zong Balboa]] (1899–1950) was administered as a dependent territory of the Kingdom of Zong. Its garrison and naval presence at Sant Cristòfor constituted the principal Zong military asset in Lurandia. Sinchew and Louyang, as commonwealth constituents, were obligated to support Zong in the event of conflict, and both had contributed forces to the Second War of the Isthmus. Sanrobonia, a northern island country in Abyala, maintained a longstanding alliance with Zong and provided naval and logistical cooperation in the Kendriyan Ocean.

Mercian Lurandia (New Hwice), a former Mercian colony, and the United Midlands, a former Mercian colonial possession, maintained close ties to Mercia. Mercia, allied with Volisania, exerted pressure on both territories to support the Volisanian war effort. From 1935, Mercian Lurandia and the United Midlands provided covert logistical and intelligence support to the Volisanian coalition.

== Prelude ==

=== The Palpoa agreement (1937) ===

In 1937, Mercia concluded an agreement with Mercian Lurandia under which Palpoa Province — then a Sinchew territory bordering both Mercian Lurandia and Balboa — would be transferred to Mercian Lurandia in the event of a successful joint military operation against the Sinchewese forces stationed there. The agreement was motivated by Mercia's strategic interest in opening a southern front against Sinchew, thereby drawing Sinchewese forces away from the isthmus and relieving pressure on the Volisanian–Balboan siege of Sant Cristòfor.

Sinchew had maintained a military garrison in Palpoa since the Second War of the Isthmus, and the province's position — directly south of the Balboan interior and adjacent to Mercian Lurandia — made it a potential staging ground for either side. The Mercian–Mercian Lurandian agreement provided the political framework for the Palpoa campaign, which opened later in 1937.

== Course of the war ==

=== Opening: the Balboa Campaign (1935) ===

{{main|Balboa Campaign}}

The Great Adonian War opened on the isthmus with the [[Balboa Campaign]] of 1935, a coordinated Volisanian–Balboan assault on Zong Balboa. The attack had been planned in secret for several years and was executed as the opening move of the wider Volisanian war effort. The Volisanian forces, supported by the re-equipped Balboan constabulary, seized the canal zone in the opening weeks, overrunning the installations at [[Portnou]] and the canal corridor.

The city of Sant Cristòfor, however, was protected by its historic fortification walls and by the Zong naval presence in the harbour. Volisanian and Balboan forces encircled the city by land but were unable to breach the walls or to establish a naval blockade sufficient to sever Zong supply lines. The siege of Sant Cristòfor accordingly persisted for the duration of the war, with the city sustained by Zong naval convoys that maintained sufficient control of the Kendriyan approaches to prevent a complete blockade.

The Balboan constabulary, which provided ground forces for the canal zone assault, was poorly trained and poorly led, and was unable to sustain offensive operations against the Zong defenders. The Volisanian forces bore the principal military burden for the remainder of the siege.

=== The Palpoa campaign (1937–1939) ===

The Palpoa campaign opened in 1937 when Mercian Lurandia launched an assault on the Sinchewese garrison in Palpoa Province. The initial attack, conducted by Mercian Lurandian forces alone, failed to dislodge the Sinchewese defenders, who were well-entrenched in the province's mountainous terrain. The failure of the initial assault prompted the formal entry of the United Midlands into the war, which in turn triggered the entry of Louyang on the Zong side. Louyang's entry opened the [[Lurandian Theater]] in the [[Great Lurandian Range]], which is treated as a separate article.

With the reinforcement of United Midlands forces, the Mercian Lurandian–United Midlands coalition renewed the offensive in 1938. The combined forces, which significantly outnumbered the Sinchewese garrison, succeeded in overrunning the principal Sinchewese positions in Palpoa by early 1939. The transfer of Palpoa to Mercian Lurandia, as stipulated by the 1937 agreement, was effected upon the capture of the provincial capital.

=== Siege of Sant Cristòfor (1935–1947) ===

{{main|Siege of Sant Cristòfor}}

The siege of Sant Cristòfor constituted the longest continuous engagement of the Isthmus War and one of the longest sieges in modern military history. The walled city, defended by the Zong garrison and sustained by naval supply lines, held out for the entire twelve-year duration of the conflict. The population endured sustained aerial bombing, food shortages, and the progressive destruction of the city's residential and commercial infrastructure.

The Zong naval convoys, which operated from bases in the Kendriyan Ocean and from Sanrobonian ports, maintained sufficient control of the sea approaches to deliver supplies, reinforcements, and civilian evacuations throughout the siege. The Volisanian navy, which was engaged on multiple fronts, was unable to concentrate sufficient forces to sever the supply lines entirely, though intermittent naval actions reduced the frequency and volume of deliveries, particularly in the later years of the war.

The civil administration, under Governor [[Ng Chiau-li]], managed rationing, civil defence, and the maintenance of essential services throughout the siege. The Deliberative Assembly continued to function under wartime constraints. The population of the city declined from approximately 2.5 million at the outbreak of war to an estimated 1.75 million by 1947, with the reduction attributable to famine, bombing, military casualties, and the evacuation of civilians by Zong naval convoys.

=== Zong–Sinchew counter-offensive (1942–1943) ===

In 1942, Zong and Sinchew launched a two-axis counter-offensive against the Volisanian coalition. The southern axis advanced through Palpoa, moving northward from Sinchew through the province and into the Balboan interior. The northern axis, staged from [[Sanrobonia]], comprised Zong naval and ground forces that conducted an amphibious and overland invasion of the northern Balboan coast, advancing southward toward the interior. The two-axis campaign, supported by Zong naval operations along the Kendriyan coast, caught the Volisanian and Balboan forces off guard: the coalition's defensive positions had been oriented toward the canal zone and the Sant Cristòfor siege, and both the interior and the northern coast were lightly defended.

The Zong–Sinchew advance was rapid on both axes. On the southern axis, Palpoa, which had been under Mercian Lurandian control since 1939, was recaptured by Sinchewese forces as part of the offensive. On the northern axis, Zong forces advancing from Sanrobonian staging bases secured the Kendriyan coastal ports and pressed inland, converging with the southern column. The province changed hands again later in the war, as the Volisanian coalition mounted counter-attacks, and the territory remained contested until the final months of the conflict.

[[Sant Bart]] was captured in 1943, and the remaining provincial centres fell in the following months. President [[Arturo Mendez]] fled to Volisania as the capital fell. The Balboan constabulary disintegrated as a fighting force, and the Zong occupation of the Balboan interior — which lasted until 1947 — was established. The occupation is treated in a separate article.

Zong naval aircraft, operating from carriers in the Kendriyan Ocean and from Sanrobonian airfields, conducted bombing raids on Sant Bart and on the road and rail infrastructure connecting the interior to the front lines. The raids intensified during the 1942 campaign, as Zong air power was used to soften the interior's defences in advance of the ground offensive.

=== Final years (1943–1947) ===

The final years of the Isthmus War were characterised by the exhaustion of all parties. The Volisanian coalition, stretched across multiple fronts, was unable to mount a significant counter-offensive to retake the Balboan interior. The Zong coalition, which had achieved its principal territorial objectives, focused on consolidating its occupation of the interior and maintaining the defence of Sant Cristòfor.

The Balboan interior, under Zong occupation from 1943, was devastated. The agricultural output had declined as the labour force was depleted by conscription and desertion; the infrastructure had been damaged by the Zong bombing raids and the ground fighting; and the population was malnourished and displaced. The Zong occupation authorities dismantled the republic's institutions and imposed military government.

Palpoa continued to change hands. Sinchewese forces recaptured the province during the 1942 counter-offensive, but Mercian Lurandian and United Midlands forces mounted counter-attacks in 1944 and 1945, regaining portions of the territory. The front lines in Palpoa stabilised by 1946, with Sinchew controlling the southern half and the Volisanian coalition the northern half.

The war ended in 1947 with the collapse of the Volisanian fascist government under liberal forces. The siege of Sant Cristòfor was lifted as part of the general cessation of hostilities. The Zong occupation of the Balboan interior was terminated, and the [[Adonian Community]] assumed administration of the former Zong Balboa territory.

== Aftermath ==

The post-war settlement addressed the disposition of the isthmus territories among the broader territorial questions of the Great Adonian War. The principal outcomes were:

* [[Palpoa Province]] was returned to Sinchew. The 1937 Mercian–Mercian Lurandian agreement was voided by the post-war settlement, and the province was restored to its pre-war status as a Sinchew territory.
* [[Zong Balboa]] was dissolved in 1950 and placed under an [[Adonian Community]] mandate. The mandate territory was returned to Balboa in 1960 under the [[Twelve Guarantees]].
* The Balboan interior, which had been under Zong military government from 1943 to 1947, reverted to Balboan sovereignty. The government that emerged from the war was weakened and discredited.
* President [[Arturo Mendez]], who had fled to Volisania in 1943, was handed over to the Adonian Community by the incoming Volisanian provisional authorities. He was convicted of war crimes by the [[AC Tribunal]] in 1949 — principally for the conscription and forced labour of the Black Balboan population during the war — and was sentenced to life imprisonment.
* The [[Balboa Canal]] zone was placed under AC mandate and was returned to Balboa in 1973 under the [[Treaty of Areza]].

The Isthmus War produced a civilian death toll estimated at approximately 2 million, the majority in the Balboan interior. The interior's population declined from approximately 16 million in 1935 to 11.7 million by 1947, a reduction of roughly 35 per cent. The demographic impact of the war — particularly the disproportionate mortality among the Black Balboan population, which bore the brunt of conscription, forced labour, and famine — shaped the post-war political settlement and the anti-discrimination provisions of the Twelve Guarantees.

